{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"source '_meta/agents/Kourovka/paths.env'; if [ -r \\\"\"'$KOUROVKA_PDF\" ]; then echo '\"'KOUROVKA_PDF readable'; else echo 'KOUROVKA_PDF UNREADABLE'; fi; if [ -n \\\"\"'$KOUROVKA_AUTHOR\" ]; then printf '\"'KOUROVKA_AUTHOR=%s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$KOUROVKA_AUTHOR\"; else echo '\"'KOUROVKA_AUTHOR EMPTY'; fi; pdftotext -f 177 -l 177 -layout \\\"\"'$KOUROVKA_PDF\" - | sed -n '\"'1,180p'; rg -n -F '21.90' 'Research/Group theory/Open problems/Kourovka' | head -30; command -v pdftoppm; command -v gap || true\"","aggregated_output":"KOUROVKA_PDF readable\nKOUROVKA_AUTHOR=operator\n                           New Problems (21st issue, 2026)                                 177\n\n21.86. (M. Gromov, B. Weiss). A group G is said to be sofic if for every finite set\nF ⊆ G containing 1 and every ε > 0 there exist n ∈ N and a map φ : F → Sn such\nthat φ(1) = 1, d(φ(gh), φ(g)φ(h)) < ε for all g, h such that gh ∈ F , φ(g) does not\nhave fixed points for every g ∈ F \\ {1}.\n   Is every group sofic?                                               A. Lubotzky\n\n\n21.87. Assume that a finite group G has a family of d-generator subgroups whose\nindices have no common divisor. Is it true that G can be generated by d + 1 elements?\n   The answer is positive if G is solvable (L. G. Kovács, H.-S. Sim, Indag. Math., 2\n(1991), 229–232). For an arbitrary finite group G it is proved that G can be generated\nby d + 2 elements (A. Lucchini, Commun. Algebra, 28, no. 4 (2000), 1875–1880).\n                                                                                  A. Lucchini\n\n\n21.88. Is there a finite non-abelian group G of odd order, with k(G) conjugacy\nclasses, such that k(G)/|G| = 1/17?                                D. MacHale\n\n\n21.89. For n > 39, is it true that the number of conjugacy classes in the symmetric\ngroup Sn of degree n is never a divisor of the order of Sn ? In other words, is it true\nthat, for n > 39, the number p(n) of integer partitions of n is never a divisor of n!?\n                                                                                  D. MacHale\n\n\n21.90. Let Γ be a graph of diameter d. For i ∈ {1, 2, . . . , d}, let Γi be the graph on\nthe same vertex set as Γ with vertices u, w adjacent in Γi if and only if dΓ (u, w) = i.\nDoes there exist a Q-polynomial distance-regular graph Γ of diameter 3 such that Γ2\nand Γ3 are strongly regular?                                              A. A. Makhnëv\n\n\n21.91. (W. Willems). Conjecture: The sum of squares of the degrees of the irreducible\np-Brauer characters of a finite group G is at least the p′ -part of |G|.\n    This is known to be true for p = 2 (G. Malle, Adv. Math., 380 (2021), Paper\nno. 107609, 15 pp.).                                                         G. Malle\n\n\n21.92. Conjecture: The number of irreducible p-Brauer characters of a finite group\nG is bounded above by the maximum of the number of conjugacy classes k(H) in\np′ -subgroups H of G.                           G. Malle, G. Navarro, G. Robinson\n\n\n21.93. (M. Herzog, J. Schönheim). Let G be a group and let k ⩾ 2. Let H1 , . . . , Hk\nbe subgroups of G, and g1 , . . . , gk elements of G such that the cosets g1 H1 , . . . , gk Hk\nform a partition of G. Is it true that |G : Hi | = |G : Hj | for some i ̸= j?\n   This is known to be true for groups with a Sylow tower (M. A. Berger, A. Felzen-\nbaum, A. Fraenkel, Fund. Math., 128, no. 3 (1987), 139–144). Also cf. 20.99.\n                                                                                  L. Margolis\n\fResearch/Group theory/Open problems/Kourovka/21.90-distance-regular-graph-distance-graphs.md:2:title: \"Kourovka Problem 21.90 — Distance Regular Graph Distance Graphs\"\nResearch/Group theory/Open problems/Kourovka/21.90-distance-regular-graph-distance-graphs.md:9:problem_id: \"21.90\"\nResearch/Group theory/Open problems/Kourovka/21.90-distance-regular-graph-distance-graphs.md:25:# Kourovka Problem 21.90 — Distance Regular Graph Distance Graphs\nResearch/Group theory/Open problems/Kourovka/21.90-distance-regular-graph-distance-graphs.md:29:> 21.90. Let Γ be a graph of diameter d. For i ∈ {1, 2, . . . , d}, let Γi be the graph on the same vertex set as Γ with vertices u, w adjacent in Γi if and only if dΓ (u, w) = i. Does there exist a Q-polynomial distance-regular graph Γ of diameter 3 such that Γ2 and Γ3 are strongly regular? A. A. Makhnëv\n/usr/bin/pdftoppm\n/usr/bin/gap\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
